1. INTRODUCTION: THE LAST SPEC

1.1 The Gap

Every previous spec ended with the same honest label: "not yet certified." A1–A8 specify properties that would satisfy norms. None of them demonstrate satisfaction to a third party.

A9 closes that gap — not by certifying, but by specifying how certification would proceed and what artifacts must exist.

1.2 The Three-Layer Chain

Certification rests on a three-layer chain:

```
Layer 1 — Formal Artifacts
  A0–A8 specs, Verifiable Agency Theorem (paper proof)
       ↓
Layer 2 — Machine Verification
  TLA+ modules, TLC model-check, TLAPS discharge
       ↓
Layer 3 — Hardware Validation
  Hexagon DSP port, RISC-V port, FPGA/ASIC prototype
       ↓
       ↓
Certification Dossier
  Traceability matrix, evidence package, third-party audit
```

Each layer depends on the one below. A dossier cannot cite a hardware result that does not exist. Hardware validation cannot proceed without a machine-checked model.

1.3 The Obligation

A9 has one obligation:

Obligation O1 (Traceability). Every certification requirement maps to a specific spec clause and a specific artifact.

If O1 fails, the dossier is incomplete. If O1 holds, the dossier is constructible.

1.4 The Design Principle

Certification is not a claim. It is a dossier. The dossier is a set of artifacts. The artifacts are already specified. A9 says where they live.

