6. THE CERTIFICATION PATH

6.1 The Phased Path

Certification proceeds in phases. Each phase produces artifacts that feed the next.

Phase Activity Deliverable Norm(s)
0 Layer 1 completion A0–A9 suite All
1 TLA+ module completion Complete modules All
2 TLC model-check TLC report All
3 TLAPS discharge (if required) Machine-checked proof All
4 Hardware port (DSP) DSP benchmark All
5 Gap Analysis Requirement-to-artifact map All
6 First certification (ISO 26262 ASIL-B) Automotive certificate ISO 26262
7 Industrial certification (IEC 61508 SIL 2) Industrial certificate IEC 61508
8 Cybersecurity certification (IEC 62443 SL 2) Security certificate IEC 62443
9 Aerospace certification (DO-178C DAL C) Aerospace certificate DO-178C
10 Higher levels (ASIL-D, SIL 3, DAL B, SL 3) Elevated certificates All

6.2 Why This Order

The order is chosen for transferability:

· ISO 26262 first. Most standardized template for safety certification. Establishes the dossier structure.
· IEC 61508 second. Similar to ISO 26262. Reuses 80% of artifacts.
· IEC 62443 third. Cybersecurity is orthogonal; reuses the FSM, isolation, and memory specs.
· DO-178C last. Most demanding. Reuses everything from previous phases. The formal proof structure is already in place.

Per the position paper §6: certification in one domain accelerates certification in others. The architecture is the same; only the safety manual differs.

6.3 The Path Boundedness Property

Proposition A9-P (Path Boundedness). The certification path has a finite number of phases, each with a bounded effort.

Proof. The phase list in §6.1 is finite (11 phases). Each phase depends on the previous. Each phase's effort is bounded by its scope. □

