APPENDIX A — Plain Language

Can you certify an AI agent?
Not the way it's done today. Today's AI is stochastic — no one can prove what it will do.
Can you certify this AI agent?
Yes. Because it's deterministic and finite, you can prove its complete behavior in advance.
How?
Three layers. First, the formal specs. Second, machine-checked proofs. Third, hardware measurements.
Are the specs done?
Yes. A0 through A9.
Are the machine proofs done?
The modules are written. The model-checking run is next.
Is the hardware done?
Not yet. That's the last layer.
When you have all three, what do you do?
You assemble a dossier and submit it to a certification body.
What do they check?
That every requirement of the norm is satisfied by a specific artifact.
And then?
You get certified. ASIL. SIL. DAL. SL.
What is this called?
A9 — verification and certification path.

